00:22:35,470 --> 00:22:36,530
And lastly, one more.

426
00:22:36,850 --> 00:22:37,810
One more.

427
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:44,220
Handling a sudden resignation from a key team member, a problem that can really throw a wrench in operations.

428
00:22:44,240 --> 00:22:45,490
Oh, yeah, that can cause chaos.

429
00:22:45,770 --> 00:22:46,810
What's the immediate action?

430
00:22:46,910 --> 00:22:48,110
Practical stuff first.

431
00:22:48,770 --> 00:22:51,450
Reassigning critical tasks temporarily to keep things moving.

432
00:22:51,780 --> 00:22:52,380
Damage control.

433
00:22:52,460 --> 00:22:53,320
Keep the ship afloat.

434
00:22:53,560 --> 00:22:59,390
Then prioritizing filling that position quickly, whether promoting someone internally or hiring externally.

435
00:22:59,670 --> 00:23:00,270
Plugging the gap.

436
00:23:00,290 --> 00:23:00,470
Right.

437
00:23:00,670 --> 00:23:02,190
But what about preventing it next time?

438
00:23:02,770 --> 00:23:03,630
Or being ready next time?

439
00:23:03,830 --> 00:23:06,120
Ah, the forward-looking step.

440
00:23:06,880 --> 00:23:11,030
Creating a backup plan for the future before another key person leaves.

441
00:23:11,790 --> 00:23:18,350
This means cross-training other team members, documenting processes thoroughly so the knowledge isn't lost with the person.

442
00:23:18,430 --> 00:23:20,250
Building that internal resilience again.

443
00:23:20,310 --> 00:23:21,150
Exactly.

444
00:23:21,490 --> 00:23:25,340
Communicating transparently with the remaining team was seen as vital, too.

445
00:23:25,820 --> 00:23:31,340
Acknowledging their efforts during the transition, maybe offering incentives or support if they're taking on extra tasks.

446
00:23:31,880 --> 00:23:34,490
It's easy to overlook the impact on the people who stay behind.

447
00:23:34,930 --> 00:23:37,290
Yeah, supporting the remaining team is crucial.

448
00:23:37,310 --> 00:23:37,750
Definitely.

449
