1006
00:48:53,660 --> 00:48:55,050
But you're parking it for later.

1007
00:48:55,190 --> 00:48:55,470
Yes.

1008
00:48:56,150 --> 00:48:59,880
You defer the detailed chat to keep the current agenda moving.

1009
00:48:59,920 --> 00:49:04,350
It stops the meeting from getting sidetracked without just dismissing someone's idea.

1010
00:49:04,670 --> 00:49:05,690
I think I saw an example.

1011
00:49:06,090 --> 00:49:10,320
Someone asks about like the Q3 budget during a client prep call.

1012
00:49:10,360 --> 00:49:14,460
Right, and the leader says something like, good point, but we're tight on time now.

1013
00:49:14,740 --> 00:49:17,200
Can we circle back to that after the client call?

1014
00:49:17,460 --> 00:49:20,260
Clear signal, not now, but definitely later.

1015
00:49:20,700 --> 00:49:22,510
I bet that saves a ton of meeting time.

1016
00:49:22,670 --> 00:49:24,690
It's incredibly useful for focus.

1017
00:49:25,010 --> 00:49:28,410
Now, related but different is, let's take this offline.

1018
00:49:28,610 --> 00:49:29,690
Okay, how's that one different?

1019
00:49:29,710 --> 00:49:35,720
This is for when a discussion is happening, maybe in that meeting, but it's getting, well, maybe too deep into the weeds.

1020
00:49:35,760 --> 00:49:38,620
Too detailed, or maybe only relevant for a couple of people.

1021
00:49:38,650 --> 00:49:39,200
Exactly.

1022
00:49:39,360 --> 00:49:40,940
Or perhaps it's a bit sensitive.

1023
00:49:40,960 --> 00:49:49,540
So let's take this offline is basically proposing to continue that specific topic later, but in a different setting, usually smaller, maybe just one on one.

1024
00:49:49,960 --> 00:49:50,400
Ah, I see.

1025
00:49:50,720 --> 00:49:55,330
Like the example where a team lead says this is getting a bit granular for the group.

1026
00:49:55,350 --> 00:49:57,780
Let's take this offline and sync up one on one later.

1027
00:49:57,860 --> 00:49:58,480
Precisely.

1028
00:49:58,520 --> 00:50:02,910
It protects the main meeting's time and focus, and maybe protects privacy, too.

1029
00:50:03,150 --> 00:50:04,410
So both are about focus.

1030
00:50:04,810 --> 00:50:08,880
But circle back means same topic, same group, later.

1031
00:50:09,340 --> 00:50:13,500
While take offline means same topic, different group, later.

1032
00:50:13,650 --> 00:50:14,730
That's a perfect summary.

1033
00:50:15,140 --> 00:50:16,160
Crucial difference.

1034
00:50:16,180 --> 00:50:17,240
Okay, now.

1035
00:50:17,260 --> 00:50:19,060
Maybe the most important part of workplace comms.

1036
00:50:19,800 --> 00:50:21,880
Making sure everyone actually understands each other.

1037
00:50:21,900 --> 00:50:22,340
Alignment.

1038
00:50:22,620 --> 00:50:23,320
Collaboration.

1039
00:50:23,590 --> 00:50:26,630
Yeah, preventing misunderstandings, getting people to work together well.

1040
00:50:27,110 --> 00:50:29,070
What phrases did the sources highlight here?

1041
00:50:29,410 --> 00:50:33,640
Key ones were, on the same page, please keep me in the loop, and one I really like.

1042
00:50:33,920 --> 00:50:34,660
That's a great point.

1043
00:50:34,680 --> 00:50:35,420
Let me build on that.

1044
00:50:35,540 --> 00:50:36,240
On the same page.

1045
00:50:37,320 --> 00:50:38,240
Seems pretty straightforward.

1046
00:50:38,380 --> 00:50:39,510
Just checking understanding.

1047
00:50:39,930 --> 00:50:40,810
Fundamentally, yes.

1048
00:50:40,970 --> 00:50:41,890
It's a quick check.

1049
00:50:42,470 --> 00:50:43,100
Do we agree?

1050
00:50:43,280 --> 00:50:44,540
Do we understand this the same way?

1051
00:50:45,040 --> 00:50:46,460
Before you proceed.

1052
00:50:46,480 --> 00:50:48,960
Stops assumptions causing problems down the line.

1053
00:50:49,300 --> 00:50:51,910
Like this simple example, just to make sure we're on the same page.

1054
00:50:52,530 --> 00:50:54,330
The client wants a draft by Tuesday.

1055
00:50:54,490 --> 00:50:54,970
Yeah.

1056
00:50:54,990 --> 00:50:55,670
Yes, correct.

1057
00:50:55,850 --> 00:50:56,470
Exactly.

1058
00:50:56,490 --> 00:51:03,620
Essential for aligning on facts, goals, next steps, especially before committing resources or making decisions.

1059
00:51:03,790 --> 00:51:04,270
Makes sense.

1060
00:51:04,970 --> 00:51:06,510
And please keep me in the loop.

1061
00:51:06,850 --> 00:51:08,450
That's about information flow.

1062
00:51:08,470 --> 00:51:09,050
Absolutely.

1063
00:51:09,090 --> 00:51:11,380
It's a direct but polite request.

1064
00:51:11,900 --> 00:51:18,100
Just asking to be kept informed about how something's progressing, even if you aren't involved in every single detail.

1065
00:51:18,280 --> 00:51:18,500
Right.

1066
00:51:18,520 --> 00:51:21,930
Maybe you delegated something or you're waiting on info that affects your own work.

1067
00:51:22,110 --> 00:51:22,410
Yeah.

1068
00:51:22,550 --> 00:51:29,690
Or like the example of a consultant saying to a client, OK, understood on those changes, please keep me in the loop so I can adjust my part accordingly.

1069
00:51:30,470 --> 00:51:31,960
So you don't get caught by surprise.

1070
00:51:32,110 --> 00:51:32,930
Exactly.

1071
00:51:32,970 --> 00:51:36,500
Let's you stay informed without constantly having to chase people for updates.

1072
00:51:36,920 --> 00:51:38,260
Now, that's a great point.

1073
00:51:38,280 --> 00:51:38,910
Let me build on that.

1074
00:51:39,430 --> 00:51:40,650
This one is really interesting.

1075
00:51:40,910 --> 00:51:42,600
Oh, why is that?

1076
00:51:43,040 --> 00:51:44,140
What makes it so powerful?

1077
00:51:44,690 --> 00:51:47,820
Well, the sources point out it does two key things at once.

1078
00:51:48,020 --> 00:51:55,350
First, it validates the person who just spoke, saying that's a great point, makes them feel heard, encourages them and others to contribute more.

1079
00:51:55,510 --> 00:51:57,770
OK, so it boosts psychological safety a bit.

1080
00:51:57,790 --> 00:51:58,230
Definitely.

1081
00:51:58,810 --> 00:52:09,270
And second, the let me build on that part frames your contribution not as a correction or a totally separate idea, but as an addition, an expansion of their idea.

1082
00:52:09,250 --> 00:52:11,030
Ah, so you're not hijacking it.

1083
00:52:11,050 --> 00:52:12,310
You're saying, good start.

1084
00:52:12,330 --> 00:52:13,370
Let's develop it together.

1085
00:52:13,510 --> 00:52:14,030
Precisely.

1086
00:52:14,280 --> 00:52:19,040
It shifts the dynamic from competitive whose idea is better to collaborative.

1087
00:52:19,300 --> 00:52:22,090
Like the example, colleague A suggests digital marketing.

1088
00:52:22,450 --> 00:52:24,070
Colleague B says, that's a great point.

1089
00:52:24,170 --> 00:52:24,970
Let me build on that.

1090
00:52:25,270 --> 00:52:27,500
We could also bring in some social media influencers.

1091
00:52:27,680 --> 00:52:30,340
It connects the ideas smoothly, creates shared ownership.

1092
00:52:30,420 --> 00:52:30,920
I like that.

1093
00:52:31,060 --> 00:52:32,180
It's very effective.

1094
00:52:32,160 --> 00:52:32,980
Okay, great stuff.

1095
00:52:33,590 --> 00:52:40,760
Finally, the sources also touched on phrases for clarifying things and politely asking for input.

1096
00:52:41,080 --> 00:52:44,500
Yes, crucial for avoiding errors and tapping into collective knowledge.

1097
00:52:45,020 --> 00:52:52,730
Key phrases mentioned were, could you clarify what you mean by, I'd like to get your input on, and as far as I'm aware.

1098
00:52:53,010 --> 00:52:53,710
Could you clarify?

1099
00:52:54,010 --> 00:52:56,560
That feels like the essential anti-ambiguity tool.

1100
00:52:56,800 --> 00:53:01,550
It's just a straightforward, polite way to say, hang on, I'm not 100% sure I follow.

1101
00:53:01,610 --> 00:53:04,110
And that's so much better than just guessing or making assumptions, right?

1102
00:53:04,130 --> 00:53:07,280
If someone says we need to optimize the workflow, you need to know what they mean.

1103
00:53:07,580 --> 00:53:07,820
Right.

1104
00:53:08,080 --> 00:53:10,080
So you can ask, could you clarify optimize?

1105
00:53:10,140 --> 00:53:13,000
Are we talking fewer steps or new software?

1106
00:53:13,240 --> 00:53:13,700
Exactly.

1107
00:53:13,960 --> 00:53:15,820
Gets everyone working on the actual problem.

1108
00:53:16,020 --> 00:53:17,040
Prevents wasted effort.

1109
00:53:17,450 --> 00:53:18,950
And I'd like to get your input on.

1110
00:53:19,610 --> 00:53:22,530
It seems like the perfect way to ask for help or someone's opinion.

1111
00:53:22,690 --> 00:53:23,150
It really is.

1112
00:53:23,190 --> 00:53:25,770
It shows you respect their expertise or perspective.

1113
00:53:26,110 --> 00:53:27,630
It invites collaboration.

1114
00:53:27,690 --> 00:53:29,680
Makes people feel valued.

1115
00:53:29,840 --> 00:53:34,070
Like saying, before we finalize this marketing strategy, I'd really like to get your input.

1116
00:53:34,270 --> 00:53:34,650
Perfect.

1117
00:53:35,090 --> 00:53:37,770
Or asking a peer to look over a draft report.

1118
00:53:38,350 --> 00:53:40,170
It leverages the team's knowledge.

1119
00:53:40,710 --> 00:53:41,430
Oh, and the last one.

1120
00:53:42,070 --> 00:53:45,720
As far as I'm aware, why this specific phrasing?

1121
00:53:46,120 --> 00:53:49,100
It's about being careful, professionally cautious.

1122
00:53:49,600 --> 00:53:57,450
You're sharing information you believe is correct, but you're implicitly acknowledging your knowledge might have gaps or might not be the absolute latest.

1123
00:53:57,500 --> 00:54:00,000
So it protects you a bit, but also manages expectations.

1124
00:54:00,020 --> 00:54:00,660
Precisely.

1125
00:54:01,010 --> 00:54:05,430
It signals, this is what I know right now, but I'm open to correction or further info.

1126
00:54:05,770 --> 00:54:10,880
Like a sales rep saying, as far as I'm aware, the shipment went out this morning, but let me double check with logistics.

1127
00:54:10,900 --> 00:54:11,900
Honest and proactive.

1128
00:54:12,340 --> 00:54:12,440
Okay.

1129
00:54:12,460 --> 00:54:12,920
Yeah.

1130
00:54:12,940 --> 00:54:23,760
So when you look at all these phrases together, touch base, reaching out, circle back, take offline, on the same page, keep me in the loop, build on that, clarify, get your input.

1131
00:54:24,140 --> 00:54:25,400
As far as I'm aware-

1132
00:54:25,870 --> 00:54:27,290
It really paints a picture, doesn't it?

1133
00:54:27,390 --> 00:54:30,360
Yeah, it shows how these aren't just random expressions.

1134
00:54:30,390 --> 00:54:33,500
They're like operational tools for communicating effectively.

1135
00:54:33,660 --> 00:54:34,700
That's a great way to put it.

1136
00:54:35,450 --> 00:54:42,170
And mastering them, as the sources show with all those examples, really can boost your clarity, your professionalism.

1137
00:54:42,330 --> 00:54:43,780
Make interactions more efficient.

1138
00:54:43,900 --> 00:54:44,600
Absolutely.

1139
00:54:44,920 --> 00:54:52,690
And help you navigate those common workplace situations with, well, just more confidence, sounding more natural and purposeful.

1140
00:54:53,030 --> 00:54:54,430
It really does make a lot of sense.

1141
00:54:54,450 --> 00:54:54,630
Yeah.

1142
00:54:55,070 --> 00:54:57,830
And the sources had a final thought on this, didn't they?

1143
00:54:58,270 --> 00:54:59,220
About practice.

1144
00:54:59,260 --> 00:54:59,740
They did.

1145
00:54:59,780 --> 00:55:05,450
They stressed that just knowing these phrases isn't enough to really make them feel natural, make them yours.

1146
00:55:05,510 --> 00:55:06,890
You actually have to use them.

1147
00:55:07,010 --> 00:55:08,060
Put them into practice.

1148
00:55:08,080 --> 00:55:08,440
Yeah.

1149
00:55:08,840 --> 00:55:10,680
They suggested actively trying them out.

1150
00:55:10,920 --> 00:55:16,050
Maybe start in low stakes situations first or even, you know, run through mock conversations.

1151
00:55:16,210 --> 00:55:20,100
Record yourself maybe or role play with a colleague you trust.

1152
00:55:20,280 --> 00:55:20,520
Right.

1153
00:55:20,600 --> 00:55:24,220
So maybe think of it as a challenge coming straight from the sources.

1154
00:55:24,200 --> 00:55:25,890
Pick just one phrase we talked about.

1155
00:55:26,230 --> 00:55:37,800
Maybe let's touch base or could you clarify something simple and then consciously try to use it this week in a real work situation where it fits, see how it feels, see what kind of response you get.

1156
00:55:38,070 --> 00:55:41,130
It might feel a bit clunky at first, maybe even a little awkward.

1157
00:55:41,210 --> 00:55:42,030
Sure, that's normal.

1158
00:55:42,610 --> 00:55:50,890
But that active practice, that's how these move from just being ideas in your head to being tools you can confidently pull out whenever you need them.

1159
00:55:51,030 --> 00:55:51,600
Well said.

1160
00:55:51,640 --> 00:55:58,390
It's about building that muscle memory for communication.
